```
@id("close_workorder_main_after_recent_human_approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_workorder"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    output.approval_outcome: Drupe::get_workorder_Output_approval_outcome::"GRANTED",
    output.approver_type: Drupe::get_workorder_Output_approver_type::"HUMAN"
  }
};

// Read-only lookups used to confirm approval. They must be permitted,
// otherwise no response is ever recorded for the close policy to rely on.
@id("allow_get_workorder_lookup")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_workorder", resource);

@id("allow_authority_get_approval_lookup")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval", resource);
```

`approve_workorder` has no permit, so the agent cannot approve work orders itself. Every other action is denied by default.

I did not use `authority_get_approval` as the proof of approval, because its `decision` and `approver_kind` fields are plain strings and I don't know their exact values. The close policy relies only on a completed `get_workorder` response, which reports a typed `approval_outcome` and `approver_type`. The agent may still call `authority_get_approval` as a lookup.

I wrote the enum values as entity literals (`Drupe::get_workorder_Output_approval_outcome::"GRANTED"`) because the schema declares those two fields as enum entities. The reference only shows quoted string literals, so if your Dogwood version rejects the entity form, the two output constraints would need to be written as plain strings instead.
